issue:
gzhttp: jitterBuffer logic hashes entire buffer indiscriminately

In `gzhttp/compress.go` the middleware supports adding a random jitter to the compressed output. The user can specify a `jitterBuffer` to ensure the jitter is calculated deterministically from the first N bytes of the response so the random jitter does not change based on how the application chunks its `Write()` calls.

The comment explicitly states:
`// Use only up to "w.jitterBuffer", otherwise the output depends on write sizes.`

However the implementation fails to enforce this. When a large initial `Write()` occurs the internal buffer `w.buf` fills up to `wantBuf` (which defaults to `max(w.minSize, 512)`). Then `startCompression` hashes the entire `w.buf` without slicing it down to `w.jitterBuffer`:

```go
if w.sha256Jitter {
    h := sha256.New()
    h.Write(w.buf) // BUG: hashes the entire w.buf which could be up to wantBuf bytes
    // Use only up to "w.jitterBuffer", otherwise the output depends on write sizes.
    if len(remain) > 0 && len(w.buf) < w.jitterBuffer {
        // ...
    }
} else {
    h := crc32.Update(0, castagnoliTable, w.buf) // BUG: same issue here
    // ...
}
```

Because `h.Write(w.buf)` writes the entire buffer indiscriminately the jitter RNG seed becomes highly dependent on whether the first `Write()` call was small or large. This defeats the purpose of the `jitterBuffer` configuration. 

To fix this it needs to be constrained like so:
```go
hashSize := len(w.buf)
if w.jitterBuffer > 0 && hashSize > w.jitterBuffer {
    hashSize = w.jitterBuffer
}
h.Write(w.buf[:hashSize])
```

